      "math_academy_id": "q-70887",
      "sequence_position": 1,
      "problem": "Given that $y=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{4}$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$",
      "worked_solution": "The derivative of the natural logarithm is the reciprocal function:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nWe apply our formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
